# D1. The number of //nolint directives in go/. The gate counts them and fails
# on a mismatch, so an exception being added is a two-line diff rather than one
# line nobody sees. Raise it deliberately, with the reason on the line the
# directive sits on.
#
# Phase 1 added four. Three are in internal/jail: the kernel takes a struct
# pointer for landlock_create_ruleset, landlock_add_rule and seccomp, and
# x/sys/unix carries no wrapper for any of the three. The fourth is the escape
# proof re-executing its own binary to build a mount boundary in a private
# namespace, which is the only way that row runs without being root.
#
# Phase 2 added ten. Five are the same shape: SQLite has one integer type and
# it is signed, so a device number, an inode number, a birth time and a byte
# offset cross into it as their bit pattern and come back unchanged. Narrowing
# them would refuse values a real filesystem hands out, which is the opposite
# of what the rule is for.
#
# Four are gosec reading an identifier rather than a value: a constant named
# after the app_password or totp_secret table holds a SQL statement, not a
# credential. The tenth is the import closing databases it only ever read.
#
# Phase 2.5 added two. One is the other direction of the same signed-integer
# crossing: reading a stored device or inode number back out of the override
# table. The other is the source-table inventory's row count, the one statement
# in the tree that names a table rather than binding it, whose name comes from
# that file's own literal map and is checked against a plain-identifier rule
# before it is used. Two more are gosec reading a variable where a path is
# expected: the data directory's lock file, whose name is this package's own
# constant, and the import's staging database, whose name is its own random hex.
# Both sit under a directory the caller handed in and neither takes a component
# from anywhere else. The third of that shape is the development host's copy of
# the control-file replacement, which the gate's own linter run never compiles
# because it runs for GOOS=linux.
#
# Phase 3 added thirty-four. The auth package's at-rest crypto and its
# credential-path queries are the bulk of them: the XChaCha20 buffers and the
# HMAC/hash writers never fail, the identifier-name G101 hits on SQL statements
# that mention a password or a secret table, the deliberately narrow
# seed/step/bit-width casts are all proven in range beside the cast, and MD4 and
# SHA-1 are fixed by the SMB and TOTP protocols, not chosen. The rest are the
# rows.Close folds that use the errors.Join pattern the store owns first.
#
# Phase 4 added four. Two are the file ETag feeding a signed nanosecond
# timestamp into a hash as its bit pattern, which is what a change token is:
# reinterpreting a mtime or ctime through a narrowing check would refuse
# nothing and prove nothing. One is the quota ledger's negated freed size,
# clamped to the signed range on the line above the cast. One is the job
# importer folding a uuid into a 63-bit id, masked before the conversion so the
# value is proven in range beside the cast.
#
# Phase 5 added seven. Four are gosec reading an identifier or a variable
# rather than a value: the setup code constant whose name contains "token",
# the app-password listing statement whose table name contains "password",
# the config file whose path is the operator's own argument, and the
# healthcheck's certificate path and dial target, both built from that same
# operator config. Two are the config read and the healthcheck's HTTP client,
# same shape. One is hmac.Hash.Write, which never fails and has no error path
# to act on.
#
# The core audit added nineteen, all the same families the phases before
# already named: hash and hmac writers that never fail, rows.Close on a fully
# read set, the device and inode numbers that cross into signed SQLite storage
# as their bit pattern and come back unchanged, the stream offsets that are
# bounded by the descriptor's own size, the rollback and close paths whose
# primary error is the answer, and the share-id widening whose narrow check
# sits on the line above.
#
# Phase 6 added seven. Three are hash.Hash.Write, which the interface documents
# as never failing and which therefore has no error path to act on. Three are
# gosec reading a conversion whose range check is on the line above or in the
# constant it came from: the two chunk settings, both bounded by the compiled-in
# floor, and the share-id widening the sweep does behind its own bound. The
# seventh is the free-space probe, where a statfs that could not run is a fact
# about the filesystem rather than a reason to refuse an upload. The eighth is
# a test fixture writing mode 640, which is the file another program wrote that
# the replacement has to keep the mode of.
#
# Phase 7 added six. One is xml.EscapeText writing into a bytes.Buffer, whose
# Write is documented never to fail. Three are the close-and-rows family the
# phases before already named: a multistatus close whose earlier write error is
# the real answer, and two rows.Close on fully read sets. Two are the device
# and inode numbers crossing into signed SQLite storage as their bit pattern,
# the same conversion the cache already does for the same reason.
#
# Phase 8 added one: the folding Caser, which is immutable and documented safe
# for concurrent use, so it is shared rather than rebuilt per name. Milestone 8c
# added two more of the same shape: the zstd encoder and decoder, both
# documented safe for concurrent use, built once rather than per block.
# Milestone 8d added five: four gosec file-inclusion warnings on segment paths
# this package builds from the operator's own index directory and a fixed name,
# and one on a test writing into its own TempDir. Milestone 8e added one: a
# test restoring a directory's execute bit so cleanup can descend into it,
# which gosec reads as a file mode.
#
# Phase 9 removed one: the orphaned static package, whose panic-on-missing-embed
# carried a forbidigo exception. The frontend now builds into the embedding
# package and a build without one leaves the route unmounted, so there is
# nothing to panic about.
#
# Milestones 9c and 9d added twenty-one, and all but two are the same two
# families the phases before already named: a close whose error has no reader,
# on job descriptors and on a control socket that is what just failed, and a
# process kill or wait where the process being already gone is the case being
# handled. The two others are gosec reading a subprocess launch: the pool execs
# this binary's own path, and a test builds a helper from constant arguments.
#
# Milestone 9e settled at this count after the socket and rename work moved into
# internal/vfs, where the keepalive and rename rules already live. What remains
# in preview is the same close-and-kill family, plus one bit-pattern hash of a
# timestamp in the cache key and two gosec subprocess warnings: the pool execs
# this binary's own path, and a test builds its helper from constant arguments.
#
# Milestones 9g and 9h added ten. Most are a measurement command reporting to a
# terminal that has no second channel to report a failed write on. The rest are
# the jail proof reaching the kernel directly: a raw openat needs AT_FDCWD as
# its bit pattern and a NUL-terminated path, because a probe that went through
# the standard library might not issue the syscall the filter is meant to stop.
#
# Milestone 10a added one: the compat wiring narrowing a stored share id, where
# a value that no longer fits one is a corrupt row rather than a truncation.
# Milestone 10d added two, both the OCS writer answering a client after the
# status is already sent, where a short write has no second channel. Milestones
# 10b and 10d added four more: the router answering after its status is sent,
# and three test type assertions whose shape the decode above them proved.
# Milestone 10g added three: a rows.Close on a fully read set, and two gosec
# readings of a statement that names a credential column rather than holding
# one.
# Milestone 11b added one: the passdb narrowing a stored key version, where the
# bound directly above the conversion is what makes it fit. Two more that the
# first draft had were removed instead, by narrowing the value once rather than
# converting it at each of three use sites.
# Milestone 11c added three: reading the operator's own certificate path, the
# only way to ask whether a system certificate pool is empty, and a test key
# that is deliberately undersized so the refusal can be proved, plus a test
# hash write that cannot fail.
# Milestone 11e added two: a nested-mount test creating its own temporary
# directory and handing it to its own child, and reading the kernel's magic
# value back from the mount that test just made, the test binary re-executing
# itself, and two reads of a test's own temporary directory.
# Phase 13 added three, all in the response differ: it verifies no certificate
# because it talks to two self-signed loopback servers, it reads its own corpus
# directory, and its request carries no context because the client's own
# timeout is the bound.
# Phase 13 added three more in the no-Landlock proof: the test binary
# re-executing itself, a filter length bounded on the line above the
# conversion, and the address the kernel takes a filter by.
# Phase 13's route work added three: two hash writes that cannot fail in the
# sign-in challenge, and one in the settings document reader.
# The archive writer and the link update added eight: six width conversions in
# the zip format, each bounded on the line above it, one statement naming a
# credential column, and one hash write that cannot fail.
# The SMB sidecar agent added thirty-one. Most are the security scanner
# reading this agent's own configured paths as untrusted input: it edits the
# system account file and promotes a configuration by design, so the paths it
# writes are its whole purpose rather than a traversal. The rest are file modes
# the scanner wants narrower than they can be, since a system account file
# every name lookup reads cannot be private, and signals to a process that has
# already exited. One more marks the example configuration test writing into
# the framework's own temporary directory, and one more the corpus scan's own
# bound, which is a positive constant the line above it checks. Two more sit in the
# single-sign-on flow test: a certificate pool holding only the test provider's
# own certificate, and that provider redirecting where it was told, which is
# what a provider does. Two more: the route checker reading the client directory
# it was pointed at, and the search stream writing an event to a response that
# is already committed.
# The repairs from the risk list added one: the index build dividing its own
# file count by its own elapsed nanoseconds to report the rate it measured,
# both of which the line above it checks are positive.
248

